  This all seems premature to me.   Rather than trying to jump
to a perfect multi-character nethack in one go (almost guaranteed
to fail) let's just continue what Nethack has been doing:
creeping slowly toward multi-character Nethack.  (Look at the
recent post of an ascension with "pets" wearing gray dragon
scale mail and wielding artifact weapons.)

  Make a new role that's wimpy except that it has more control
over one pet.  If pacifists can ascend with pets as they 
currently are, even a wimpy class should be able to ascend with
more control over a single pet.  Maybe just call it the pacifist
class.  

  - Starts with spellbook of charm monster.  (Implies enchantment
    spells are its speciality.)
  - Quest -
    - Artifact (Gandhi's glasses?) -
      - when invoked makes monsters peaceful.  
      - when applied acts like a magic whistle.
    - Leader: Gandhi
    - Nemesis: Genghis Khan(?)

  - At some character level(s) gets the following commands to use
    on its primary pet (How primary is determined needs to be 
    worked out.):  

    - pickup
    - drop
    - attack
    - heel  (i.e. "Leave that foocubus alone!"
    - #monster

  - Killing a monster personally gives alignment hit.  Doesn't 
    have to be big, since the pacifist can't raise alignment by 
    killing monsters.  (Okay, killing peaceful monsters gives big
    alignment hit.)  (How do pacifists raise their alignment 
    anyway?)

  - Hitting a hostile monster gives a "You caitiff!" type message
    with small alignment hit.

  The rest of the class could be based on the healer class (I 
believe the most popular class for pacifists).


  As for balance, pacifism may well be enough of a challenge
by itself.  Play testing would help determine balance issues.
(NOT BY MARVIN!)


  This small step to multi-character has enough problems of its 
own to solve.  Whether people play it, and how they play it, will
tell us more about the viability of multi-character than all our 
theorizing.



  Multi-player can then be left to later, or to players and/or 
patch authors to implement.  Hot seating isn't necessary.  My 
first exposure to Rogue (in 1980 or so) was watching two people 
sitting side-by-side at a terminal.  No hotseating.  They may not
even have swapped the keyboard back and forth.


  Note that having a Nethack expert at the keyboard would allow
a Nethack Novice to "play" the primary pet.  The novice could
say what they wanted to do and then the player at the keyboard
could type the arcane strings necessary to make happen.  
("But daddee, I wanna get the gold!")


  The emphasis is on small changes in a direction Nethack is 
already going. (magic whistle, leash, tripe rations for training,
polymorphing pets into more powerful forms, multiple pets, 
equipping pets with special armor/weapons, pacifism, etc.)

  This rather than trying a giant leap in a direction no two 
people can agree on anyway.

  Yes, there will be problems farther down the road (but only
if this trial succeeds).  Yes it's interesting and worthwhile
to try to anticipate those problems and work out solutions.

  The history of the game's development, from Rogue to Hack to 
Nethack, has been of much smaller changes than the giant leap 
from single-player single-character to multi-character 
multi-player.


